<!--
    Cast campaign, lever `conststr`: a character-pointer constant passed to a
    C library function that reads it only as a string prints as that string
    where upstream's literal falls through to the address.

        w_end: strcmp(s, digits + 15) + 1
               the NUL that ends "0123456789abcde", followed by a table, so
               emptystrconst declined the empty literal; strcmp reads ""
        w_set: strcspn(s, "\x81\x88") * 5
               not valid UTF-8, so the string manager refused it
        w_ov:  take(ov) + strlen(ov)
               ov holds overlong and surrogate UTF-8 (c1 81, e0 81 81,
               ed a0 80); strlen's literal spells all ten bytes, and take, a
               user function, keeps the address

    The same kinds of bytes keep their address in both passes where the use
    is not a library string parameter: w_count (clang -O2) passes digits + 15
    to a user function that compares it as an end pointer, so a "" would be
    another object; w_hsum passes a table with a zero byte to a (char *, len)
    user function; w_quote returns gnulib's GB18030 quotes. Four more
    controls: w_buf passes a writable .data buffer; w_word and w_name hand a
    callee whose parameter points at an unknown word the address of a long
    and of a char * they then read directly; w_wide reads the storage it
    passes at another width.

    The fixtures are non-PIE x86-64 ELFs whose file-scope data has no symbol
    (linked with -x); each pass loads the gcc -O2 build, then the clang -O2
    one. The user callees' prototypes are declared with `parse line` so the
    constants' types do not depend on the order of decompilation. Pass 1
    (`option conststr off`) is the bug and pass 2 the default.

    Source: decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/conststr_x86_64.c
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
